Combi-metal organic framework (Combi-MOF) of ${\bf a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase for one pot starch hydrolysis Manish Salgaonkar, Shamraja S. Nadar, Virendra K. Rathod
Abstract
The multi-enzyme biocatalyst allows to run in vitro multi-step cascade reactions in a single pot. An efficient combi-metal organic frameworks (combi-MOF) of $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase for one pot starch hydrolysis was constructed by mixing zinc acetate and 2- methylimmidazole with enzyme mixture in one pot under biocompatible conditions. The prepared combi-MOF was characterized and analyzed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Thermo-stability was evaluated for combi-MOF in the range of 55 to $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which showed three folds improved stability in terms of half-life. In kinetic parameter studies, rate of starch hydrolysis $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ ) of combi-MOF was found to be enhanced after coimmobilization. Further, combi-MOF was recycled in batch mode which retained upto $52\%$ residual activity after five successive cycles of reuse. In addition to that, combi-MOF exhibited extraordinary storage stability till 24 days. At the end, starch hydrolytic activity of combi-MOF was tested for different sources of starch (corn, rice, wheat and potato) which exhibited higher rate of hydrolysis than mixture of free enzymes due to spatially co-localized multi-enzymatic systems.
Key words: $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto\mathbf{\alpha}.$ -amylase, glucoamylase, combi-MOF, starch hydrolysis, one pot, glucose.
## Introduction
The exploration of enzyme as a green catalyst instead of traditional chemical catalysts for facilitating chemical/bio transformations in industries has been extensively investigated due to their sustainable, selective and high efficient nature [1–3]. In multi-enzymatic cascade systems, a series of sequential proceeding enzymatic reactions have attracted great attention due to their wide spectra of applications in the synthesis of fine chemicals, diagnostic kits and in fuel cells [4–6].
In food industries, $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase (EC 3.2.1.1) and glucoamylase (EC 3.2.1.3) are commonly employed for synergistic hydrolysis of starch to produce glucose syrup. The process of enzymatic starch hydrolysis is divided into two steps. α-Amylase is used to hydrolyze $\mathfrak{a}$ (1-4) glycosidic bond of starch called “Liquefaction process” which takes place at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by “saccharification” carries out in which glucoamylase hydrolyses $\mathfrak{a}$ (1–6) and $\mathfrak{a}$ (1–4) glycosidic bonds to produce the non-reducing ends [7,8]. However, the use of soluble (liquid) form of enzymes leads to not only the waste but also difficult recovery and reusability of enzymes. Enzyme immobilization on solid supports has been adapted as an effective tool to improve shortcomings of free enzyme. A wide range of solid carrier/supports have been employed for immobilization of enzymes, such as organic micro-particles and porous or nonporous inorganic supports, sol gels and hydrogels. The immobilization of enzyme greatly enhanced the enzyme properties like selectivity, operational stability (thermal stability, pH stability and tolerance to organic solvents) and activity to meet the demands of practical uses. It also facilitates the efficient recovery from reaction mixture and re-use of the enzyme for multiple cycles, thus enabling its cost-effective use and making them industrially feasible [9– 11]. Besides the potential benefits, immobilization of enzyme is more complicated as it is associated with several disadvantages, including tedious protocol of carrier synthesis and its functionalization, very low enzyme loading on the surface of non-porous systems and denaturation of enzymes during immobilization procedure [12,13]. More recently, mesoporous silica nanoparticles (MSN) have been demonstrated as promising enzyme supports as they have very ordered structure with high porosity, large and uniform pore size. However, inadequate interactions between the inner channel surface of MSN and enzyme molecules results in significant leaching during reaction and re-use for multiple cycle. Furthermore, enzymes are aggregated inside the channel which drastically affects the overall catalytic efficiency of biocatalyst [14–16]. These limitations hinder the use of MSN as a support for enzymes.
Recently, organic–inorganic hybrid materials (such as metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and nanoflowers) have gained considerable attention as an immobilization platform [17–19]. MOF, a fascinating class of highly porous, crystalline possesses some interesting intrinsic features, such as high surface areas, giant porosity, large pore volumes, easy tenability of pore sizes, well-defined cavities and designable organic ligands [20–22]. Also, MOFs form strong interactions between the organic counter part of the organic frameworks and proteins which prevent them from leaching out MOF platform during application. These unique properties make them a potential matrix for immobilization as compared to other inorganic supports [23–25]. In this regard, Ma and co-workers demonstrated the successful immobilization of microperoxidase-11 (MP-11) enzyme into a mesoporous MOF (TbmesoMOF) which verified better catalytic activity over MSN counterparts. After immobilization of MP-11,  Tb-mesoMOF helped to prevent undesirable aggregation of enzyme which resulted into higher reaction rate and conversion rate [26]. Lyu and co-helpers have introduced zeolitic imidazolate framework 8 (ZIF-8) to encapsulate cytochrome c by mixing enzyme solution with a methanol solution of zinc nitrate, 2-ethylimidazole along with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, as stabilizer) [27]. This method has further been used to immobilize various enzymes such as thermophilic lipase (Alcaligenes sp.) and horseradish peroxidase (HRP) into MOF supports like ZIF-8 and ZIF-10 [28,29]. More recently, our group have successfully immobilized glucoamylase and lipase (Aspergillus niger) within ZIF-8 under biocompatible conditions by using water as a solvent to prevent enzyme denaturation during biocomposite preparation. Further, these enzyme-MOF biocomposite exhibited excellent thermal stability and long-term storage stability [30,31].
In an attempt to mimic the biosynthetic efficiencies of nature and in a search for greener, more sustainable alternatives for starch hydrolysis, we report co-immobilization of $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase fabricated as combi-MOF with starch hydrolytic activity. In the coimmobilization system, the reaction intermediates could rapidly reach the next active site for the next reaction, so that the overall activity was improved. Also, these combi-MOF will help to perform multi-step reaction in a single pot without separation of intermediates. Such ‘telescoping’ of cascade enzymatic process into a one-pot catalytic process has several amenities such as smaller reactor volumes, fewer unit operations, less solvent, shorter reaction times, higher volumetric and space time yields with less waste generation – which translates to substantial commercial and environmental benefits [32–34]. Multiple enzymeincorporated MOF (zeolitic imidazolate frameworks, ZIF-8) can be readily prepared via a coprecipitation procedure (in the presence of PVP) in aqueous solution under mild condition. The multi-enzyme combi-MOF was characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR). The size, shape, morphology was analyzed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The kinetic parameters such as $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\DeltaV_{max}}}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ were evaluated for combi-MOF and free form of enzyme. Thermal stability of free and combi-MOFs was determined in terms of thermal deactivation constant $\mathrm{(k_{d})}$ , half-life $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right)$ and deactivation energy $\mathrm{(E_{d})}$ . Further, one pot hydrolysis of different starch (such as maize/corn, rice, wheat and potato starch) was carried out in batch mode using combi-MOFs. At the end, reusability studies and storage stability studies were carried out for combi-MOFs.
## Materials and methods
## Materials
$\mathfrak{a}$ -Amylase (38U) from Bacillus amyloliquefaciens (Novozymes™ BAN™ 240L) and glucoamylase (45U) from Aspergillus niger (Novozymes™ AMG 300L™) were purchased from Sigma, St. Louis, USA. Zinc acetate, maltodextrin, maize/corn starch (mesh size 60), rice starch, wheat starch (mesh size 60) and potato starch were purchased from HiMedia Laboratories Pvt. Ltd., Mumbai, India. 2-methylimmidazole and 3, 5-Dinitrosalicylic acid (DNSA) were obtained from S.D. Fine Chemicals, Mumbai, India. Glucose estimation GODPOD kit was purchased from Accurex Biomedical Pvt. Ltd., Mumbai, India. Polyvinylpyrrolidone Commercial (PVP K-30) was obtained from Sisco Research Laboratories Pvt. Ltd. (SRL), Mumbai, India. All other chemicals were of AR grade and procured from reliable sources.
## $\mathbf{a}$ -Amylase and Glucoamylase activity assay
Glucoamylase activity was determined by glucose oxidase-peroxidase (GOD-POD) enzymatic method on the basis of glucose released by hydrolysis of maltodextrin [35]. Briefly, maltodextrin ( $1\%$ w/v in $10~\mathrm{\mM}$ phosphate buffer, $\mathrm{pH}~7.4\AA,$ ) was added to a fix amount of glucoamylase and incubated for 10 min at optimum temperature. Then, the reaction was stopped by carboxylic acid (1 mL, $5\%$ w/v). Above reaction mixture $(30~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added to working solution of glucose estimation kit and kept for half hour at room temperature $(\sim30{\pm}2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in dark condition. Further, to quantify the amount of released glucose, the optical density of coloured (pink) solution was measured spectrophotometrically at $505\mathrm{nm}$ by using UV-Vis spectrophotometer (Hitachi U2001, Japan).
$\mathfrak{a}$ -Amylase activity was measured by using potato starch ( $1\%$ w/ v in $10\mathrm{\mM}$ phosphate buffer of $\mathrm{pH}~7.4\AA.$ ) as a substrate. The released reducing sugar by $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase  was estimated by using 3,5-dinitrosalicylic acid (DNS) method and expressed in terms of glucose equivalent [36,37].
One unit (U) enzyme activity is defined as the amount of enzyme essential to release one micro-mol product per unit time at optimum $\mathrm{\pH}$ and temperature.
## Preparation of combi-MOF
The combi-MOF embedded with $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase was synthesizing by the method reported by Liang and co-workers [38]. Typically, zinc acetate solution (131 mg, 40 mM, $15\ \mathrm{\mL}$ ) was added to an aqueous solution mixture of 2-methylimidazole (197 mg, 160 mM, $15~\mathrm{\mL}$ ) containing enzyme mixture (3 mg/mL α-amylase (38U) and 9 mg/mL glucoamylase (45U)) and PVP K-30. The prepared solution mixture was stirred at room temperature $(30{\pm}2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for 30 min. The mixture then immediately turned opaque and precipitation (combi-MOF) was collected by centrifuging $(8000\mathrm{\rpm},4^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . After washing with DI water for six time, the product was lyophilized and used for further experiments.
The pure MOF ZIF-8 crystals were prepared by using same procedure as that of combi-MOF but without addition of enzymes. The obtained ZIF-8 MOF was dried and used for characterization and comparison purpose.
## Immobilization yield
The immobilization yield (IY) is defined as the ratio of the amount of enzyme immobilized to total amount of enzyme used. The protein concentration in the supernatant after centrifugation was measured by using Bradford’s protein assay considering BSA as standard [39]. Briefly, Bradford’s reagent (5 mL) was added to a test tube, which contained supernatant (100 µL). After 5 min incubation, the absorption was measured by UV/Vis spectrophotometer at $595~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ The immobilization yield $(\%)$ was calculated using following equation:
$$
\%I Y=\frac{\mathrm{Immobilized~amount~of~enzyme}}{\mathrm{Total~amount~of~enzyme~used}}\times100\
$$
## Characterization of prepared combi-MOF
## Determination of properties of combi-MOF
Thermal stability of prepared combi-MOFs was studied in the temperature range of $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and expressed in form of kd, $\mathbf{t}_{1/2}$ and $\mathrm{E_{d}}$ . The thermal stability parameters were calculated by using Arrhenius equation which is given below:
$$
k=A e^{\frac{E d}{R T}}
$$
Where, k is the rate coefficient, A is a constant, $\mathrm{E_{d}}$ is the deactivation energy, R is the universal gas constant, and $\mathrm{\DeltaT}$ is the temperature (in kelvin).
The Michaelis constant $(\mathsf{K}_{\mathrm{m}})$ and the maximum reaction velocity $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ were evaluated for combi-MOF as well as for free from of $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase separately.
$$
{\frac{1}{v}}={\frac{{\mathrm{Km}}+{\mathrm{[S]}}}{{\mathrm{Vmax~}}{\mathrm{[S]}}}}
$$
Where, v is rate of reaction, [S] is concentration of substrate, $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is Michaelis-Menten constant and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{max}}}$ is maximum reaction rate.
## Starch hydrolytic activity of combi-MOF
To assess the starch hydrolysis catalytic abilities, different sources of starch such as maize/corn, rice, wheat and potato starch suspension $\langle5\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ in $10\mathrm{\mM}$ phosphate buffer of $\mathrm{pH}~7.4)$ were mixed with free enzyme mixture and combi-MOF in shaking flask at optimum condition. The starch hydrolysis was carried out till $15~\mathrm{min}$ and rate of starch hydrolysis was determined in terms of glucose release (mg) per min by using GOD-POD glucose estimation kit [8].
## Results and discussion
## Preparation of Combi-MOF
Co-immobilized multi-enzymatic systems are considered beneficial because they not only provide high rate of reaction, better output, decreased cost of procedure but also they can be easily recovered from the reaction mixture [40]. For starch hydrolysis, the $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase were simultaneously embedded within MOF in single pot co-precipitation method. In this particular method, aqueous solution of zinc solution was added to mixture containing 2-methylimidazole and enzyme mixture ( $3\ \mathrm{\mg/mL}$ $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase (38U) and $9\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ glucoamylase (45U)). The ratio of both enzymes was optimized which is shown in Figure S1. When 2-methylimidazole solution was added to zinc solution containing enzyme, the enzyme molecules efficiently induced formation of metal-organic framework and the mixture solution instantaneously (within 25 s) became opaque (brownish colour due to high concentration of enzyme solution, Figure 1). $\mathfrak{a}$ -Amylase and glucoamylase were successfully encapsulated within MOF with immobilization yield of $99\%$ (negligible amount of protein was detected in supernatant), combi-MOF product yield of $68\%$ and they exhibited activity of 73.3U per mg (determined in terms of glucose released). Further, the immobilized enzymes were lyophilized and used as Combi-MOF.
## Characterization of combi-MOF
The prepared combi-MOF was analyzed and characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR), Fourier Transform Raman (FT
Raman), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and scanning electron microscope (SEM). The XRD pattern of the pure MOF ZIF-8 (without enzyme) and combi-MOF are presented in Figure 2a-b. The prominent reflections at $26=7.27^{\circ}$ , $10.2^{\circ}$ , $12.6^{\circ}$ , $14.6^{\circ}$ , $16.3^{\circ}$ , $17.8^{\circ}$ , $21.9^{\circ}$ , $24.3^{\circ}$ , $25.4^{\circ}$ , $26.4^{\circ}$ , $29.4^{\circ}$ , $30.3^{\circ}$ for prepared MOF without enzyme correspond to simulated pure ZIF-8 crystal profile (Figure S2) with SOD-type structures. The combi-MOF showed similar PXRD patterns which indicates that crystal structure remain unaffected even after encapsulation of enzyme within it. This consistency of $X$ -ray diffraction patterns confirms that immobilization of enzyme does not alter physico-chemical properties of MOF [41,42].
The Raman spectra of pure ZIF-8 is shown in Figure 2c. The Raman spectra showed the appearance of very strong bands at $174.3~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to stretching of $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ . After immobilization of enzymes within ZIF-8 (Figure 2d), the Raman spectra showed the a new characteristic bands at 1654 and $1318~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to amide I band and amide III band along with very appearance of strong peaks at 210.6, 687.8, 1142.4 and $1457.4~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to stretching of $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ , imidazole ring puckering, $\displaystyle{\mathrm{C}}{}_{5}{-}\mathrm{N}$ stretching and methyl bending, respectively [31]. In FT-IR spectrum of pure ZIF-8 MOF (Figure 2e), the fingerprint region of 755.8 and $686.7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated with the 2-methylimidazole ring out-of-plane bending, whereas characteristic peaks in the region between 994.6 and $1305.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ arise from the in-plane bending. Additionally, two weak peaks of aliphatic and aromatic $\mathrm{{C-H}}$ stretching of the 2-methylimidazole was observed at 2927.3 and $3124.8~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ respectively [43,44]. Further, in Figure 2f, S3 and S4, the representative $\mathrm{\bfN-H}$ bending and $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching band (amide band I) of enzyme was observed at the position of around $1658.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . These IR results confirmed successful immobilization of enzymes within MOF [45].
Further, the presence of fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) tagged enzymes within ZIF-8 was confirmed by fluorescent probe under confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM). (Figure 2g, left column, single channel mode of CLSM). The confocal microscopic image showed that tagged enzyme molecules (green) were homogenously distributed within the MOF ZIF-8 crystals. The morphological study of prepared combi-MOF was done by scanning electron microscope (SEM). SEM images of combi-MOF showed the standard rhombic dodecahedral structure with ${\sim}800{\cdot}900$ nm size (Figure 2h-i). Furthermore, thermo gravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out to quantify the amount of protein (enzyme) encapsulated within ZIF-8 MOF which is presented in Figure S5. The first-stage decomposition till $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the enzymeMOF composite and pure ZIF-8 MOF exhibited steady weight loss upto ${\sim}24.5\%$ which was the result of removal of majorly water molecules and other organic small guest molecules from the MOF cavities. In the second stage of decomposition from $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $420^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , approximately $11.4\%$ of weight loss occurred which was in accordance with the decomposition of enzymes within MOF [46]. These results confirm the successful immobilization of enzymes within ZIF-8 MOF.
To investigate whether enzyme molecules are the presence on the surface or are embedded within combi-MOF, a calcination process was carried out at $420~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\textrm{h}$ to remove enzyme molecules from the enzyme-MOF (the calcination temperature was determined based on the thermal gravimetric analysis). The surface morphology of combi-MOF after calcination was captured by SEM which showed the existence of small cavities with a size ranging from 5 to $20\ \mathrm{~nm}$ (Figure 2j-k). The formation of small cavities resulted from the removal of enzyme molecules [27]. Form this SEM image analysis, we can identify that most of the protein molecules were embedded on the surface region of MOF crystals.
## Thermal kinetics studies
At higher temperatures, enzyme undergoes disruption of different interactions and bonds which distorts structural conformational resulting into permanent loss of bioactivity. The amended thermostability would possibly retain the enzyme activity and has potential to cover wide spectra of industrial application [47]. The thermal stability of free enzymes and combiMOF of $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase were evaluated at temperature range of $55\mathrm{-}75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ til one hour and checked for their residual activity after every $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ time interval (Figure 3a-c). The half-life $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right)$ and deactivation rate constants $\mathrm{(k_{d})}$ were calculated and summarized in Table 1 for free and immobilized form. The deactivation constant i.e. $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{d}}$ of immobilized combi-MOF was much lower as compared to free $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase at respective temperature. Particularly, at higher temperature i.e. $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , it was seen that the free form of both $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase was denatured and they lost their activity completely within exposure time of 40 min. On the other hand, combi-MOF showed excellent thermal stability in the range of $55\mathrm{-}75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It exhibited 4.21, 3.23 and 2.63 folds augmented half-life with respect to free enzymes at 55, 65 and $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. This enhanced thermostability can be ascribed to the internment of enzyme molecules within ZIF-8 MOF which prevents denaturation and aggregating of enzymes [48,49]. The lower half-life were exhibited by previously reported glucoamylase in the form of organic–inorganic hybrid nanoflowers as compared with our combi-MOF [50,51].
Further, linear fit Arrhenius plot was used to estimate deactivation energy $\mathrm{(E_{d})}$ for immobilized and free forms of enzymes (Figure 2c). The higher $\mathrm{E_{d}}$ values indicates higher thermal stability of enzyme [52,53]. From Table 1, it can be seen that $39.77~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ and $62.13~\mathrm{{\kJ\mol^{-1}}}$ energy was necessary to deactivate enzymes in free form (average $\mathrm{E_{d}}$ of $\mathfrak{a}$ - amylase and glucoamylase) and combi-MOF, respectively. The combi-MOF required much higher energy to alter 3D structure and destabilize the active conformation. This result could be attributed to the framework shell around $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase which provides shielding effect to preserve the active structure of enzymes in unfavourable surroundings. These outcomes conclude that structural stability is highly improved after encapsulation of both enzymes within frameworks by co-precipitation processes due to ordered MOF superstructures [30,54]. Similar results was seen for lipase after immobilization within ZIF-8 which showed that $17.5~\mathrm{kJ~}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ higher energy was required to deactivate enzyme in the range of $55\mathrm{-}75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as compared to free lipase [31].
## Michaelis–Menten kinetic parameters
Michaelis–Menten kinetic parameters of free and co-immobilized MOF were calculated by non-linear regression fitting of the initial rate of reaction with respect to varying amounts of starch as substrate. As shown in Table $2,\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ value increased after immobilization within MOF. This showed that the affinity of enzymes slightly decreased for the substrate which could be possibly due to the localization of enzyme inside the MOF which limited accessibility for starch (macro-molecular substrate) [46,55,56].
Further, $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{max}}$ values of combi-MOF of $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase were found to be higher than that of free enzyme mixture. It implied that the starch hydrolysis rate was improved after co-immobilization of enzymes within MOF. This might be due to the compartmentalization of both enzymes in the MOF which can undergo cascade reaction more efficiently. Additionally, the reduced mass transfer limitation of substrates and intermediate product leads to improved hydrolysis rate as compared to free form [57,58]. From above results, it is worth mentioning that when enzymes are locked in close proximity within MOF which can lead to reduced obstructive effects like product inhibition and unfavourable kinetics. Similar results were reported by Jadhav and Singhal for covalently conjugated alpha-amylase and glucoamylase for starch hydrolysis. They claimed that close proximity of enzyme molecules enables improves the rate of reaction [59].
## One pot starch hydrolysis derived from different sources
Combi-MOF and free enzymes mixture of $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase were tested for their starch hydrolytic efficiency under optimum conditions by using different sources of starch such as maize/corn, rice, wheat and potato starch (Table 3). Combi-MOF showed higher amount of glucose released per min from starch hydrolysis than that of free enzymes mixture. It indicated that combi-MOF exhibited higher starch hydrolytic activity which might be due to spatially co-localized multi-enzymatic systems which attributed to the fact that MOF platform offers a way to facilitate the mass transfer between the intermediate product and next active site for the next reaction, and thus allows combi-MOF to manifest the overall improved activity as compared to their native enzymes. Similar results were reported for coimmobilized enzymatic system of tri-enzyme mixture (i.e. pullulanase, alpha amylase and glucoamylase) onto glutaraldehyde activated magnetic nanoparticles (MNPs) for starch hydrolysis by Talekar and co-helpers [60].
## Reusability studies
The reusability enzyme is a key factor for any industrial use and economic point of view. The combi-MOF was recycled for 5 cycles in batch mode and residual activity was measured by hydrolyzing starch. After five successive cycles of reuse, combi-MOF retained $52\%$ of activity with respect to $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ cycle (Figure 4a). The lowering of residual activity during the recycling assay might be due to the mechanical damage and enzyme deactivation. Additionally, the recovery of combi-MOF from reaction mixture is not easy due to lower density and hence, loss of biocatalyst was observed during the centrifugation and washing of each step. However, the protein content (enzyme) was not leached out of the MOF platform and was not detected in the supernatant even after 5 operative cycles. These attributes are of great significance as they avert the purification step, thereby reducing the overall process cost [61,62].
## Storage stability studies
In the development of biocatalyst, the long-term storage stability of enzymes is another important aspect. The free $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase, glucoamylase and combi-MOF were incubated in sodium phosphate $\mathrm{(pH~}7.4\AA)$ ) buffer at room temperature $(\sim30{\pm}2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Storage stability was determined upto twenty four days by measuring the enzyme activity with four days of interval which is shown in Figure 4b. It was found that combi-MOF showed slight decrease in residual activity upto $89\%$ after 24 days. In contrast, $\mathfrak{a}$ -amylase and glucoamylase in free form showed gradual reduction in activity viz. $55\%$ and $59\%$ respectively. This might be due to robust coating layers provided by ZIF-8 frameworks around enzymes which resulted into minimal possible distortion effects of the active sites of enzyme under buffer solution (external environment). These results emphasized its higher chemical and structural stability [40,54].
## Conclusion
In summary, the combi-MOF of $\mathbf{\alpha}_{\mathbf{a}}.$ -amylase and glucoamylase was successfully synthesized via co-precipitation process in one pot under water based mild conditions. Co-immobilized enzymes within MOF showed remarkable thermal stability (between temperature ranges of $55\mathrm{-}75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and catalytic efficiency $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ . The residual activity of combi-MOF was retained up to $52\%$ after five cycles of reuse. Also, it exhibited $89\%$ residual activity after 24 days of storage which is much higher as compared to its free form. Further, combi-MOF showed excellent starch hydrolytic efficiency for different sources of starch such as maize/corn, rice, wheat and potato starch. On whole, multi-enzyme MOF system has great potential in one-pot cascade reactions and holds promise for future developments in this field.
